// Claw OS update downgrade protection.
//
// APT proves *authenticity*: the repository signature says the
// publisher produced these bytes. It does not prove *freshness* — an
// artifact that was validly signed two years ago is still validly
// signed today. The hook below compares every Claw OS candidate in a
// transaction against this machine's monotonic security floor before
// dpkg unpacks anything, so a stale mirror, a preserved repository
// snapshot or an explicit `apt install claw-os-agent=<old-version>`
// is refused rather than quietly reinstating a fixed vulnerability.
//
// The command must be a single executable token: APT looks the hook's
// protocol version up under `DPkg::Tools::Options::<command>::Version`
// using the command string verbatim, so a shell fragment here would
// register a version for a key nothing matches and APT would fall back
// to the version 1 protocol (bare filenames, no versions).
//
// /usr/lib/cos/apt/security-floor-hook decides for itself what to do
// when the verifier is absent: on a genuine pre-install bootstrap it
// drains APT's package list and succeeds, and once this machine has a
// security floor it refuses instead.
//
// Removing this file does not disable the protection. The Debian
// maintainer scripts and the Claw OS binaries enforce the same floor
// independently; this hook only moves the refusal earlier, to before
// the first file is unpacked.

DPkg::Pre-Install-Pkgs { "/usr/lib/cos/apt/security-floor-hook"; };
DPkg::Tools::Options::/usr/lib/cos/apt/security-floor-hook::Version "2";

// Freshness of the repository metadata itself. `Valid-Until` in the
// signed Release file bounds how long a mirror may keep serving an old
// index; these keep APT's own checks on and refuse the insecure
// fallbacks that would otherwise accept it.
Acquire::Check-Valid-Until "true";
Acquire::AllowInsecureRepositories "false";
Acquire::AllowDowngradeToInsecureRepositories "false";
